    content: "Article 300A provides that no person shall be deprived of his property save by authority of law. The right to property was originally a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(f) and Article 31, but successive amendments — notably the Twenty-fifth (1971) and the Forty-fourth (1978) — progressively limited it, culminating in the Forty-fourth Amendment deleting Articles 19(1)(f) and 31 and inserting Article 300A. Property is now a constitutional right, not a fundamental right: laws depriving property cannot be challenged under Article 32, but they remain reviewable under Article 14 and Article 300A itself for arbitrariness or absence of legal authority. Article 31B and the Ninth Schedule, which immunised land-reform laws from fundamental-rights challenge, were curtailed in I. R. Coelho (2007), which subjected post-1973 Ninth Schedule laws to basic-structure review. The compensation battles from Bela Banerjee (1954) onward shaped this history of property in Indian constitutional law.",
