    content: "The basic structure doctrine holds that while Parliament's amending power under Article 368 is wide, it cannot alter or destroy the Constitution's fundamental framework. Formulated in Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973) by a thirteen-judge bench deciding seven to six, the doctrine drew on German jurisprudence and the earlier Indian cases of Shankari Prasad (1951) and Golak Nath (1967). Elements identified as basic structure include the supremacy of the Constitution, the democratic and republican character of the polity, secularism, separation of powers, judicial review, federalism, the rule of law, free and fair elections, and the harmony between fundamental rights and directive principles. Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975) first applied the doctrine, invalidating the Thirty-ninth Amendment's protection of the Prime Minister's election; Minerva Mills (1980) struck down clauses 4 and 5 of Article 368 themselves; I. R. Coelho (2007) extended review to Ninth Schedule laws added after 1973.",
