    content: "Olga Tellis v. Bombay Municipal Corporation (1985) concerned pavement and slum dwellers facing eviction before the Maharashtra Assembly's monsoon session. Journalist Olga Tellis and others challenged section 314 of the Bombay Municipal Corporation Act, 1888, under which the Corporation ordered the removal of pavement dwellings. A five-judge bench held that the right to life under Article 21 includes the right to livelihood, since no person can live without the means of living: evicting pavement dwellers would deprive them of livelihood and therefore demanded fair procedure, notice and an opportunity to be heard. The Court nonetheless balanced public interest, accepting that dwellers cannot claim a right to erect structures on public pavements, while insisting that State power be exercised justly, reasonably and without arbitrariness. The case is a cornerstone of socio-economic rights jurisprudence under Article 21.",
