    content: "The Police Act, 1861 was enacted months after the 1857 revolt, on the report of the 1860 Police Commission, to create a centralised, hierarchical force serving colonial rule rather than the public. It vested superintendence of the police in the provincial government under an Inspector General and established the rank structure — superintendents, inspectors, constables — that persists today. Its philosophy prioritised State security over service, with accountability resting inside the force rather than with citizens. Most States still govern policing under this Act or successors modelled on it. The National Police Commission of 1977-81 and the Supreme Court in Prakash Singh v. Union of India (2006) pressed for reform — State Security Commissions, fixed tenure for police chiefs, separation of investigation from law-and-order — with partial implementation. Under the Act and police rules, district forces regulate assemblies, processions and public meetings through a licensing and route-permission system central to protest governance.",
