    title: "Preventive detention laws — Article 22, NSA and its lineage",
    content: "Preventive detention — detaining a person to prevent future offences rather than to punish past ones — is unusually constitutionalised in India: Article 22(3) to (7) permits it for reasons of State security and public order, subject to advisory boards of persons qualified to be High Court judges, with detention beyond three months requiring board approval. The Preventive Detention Act, 1950 lapsed in 1969, followed by the Maintenance of Internal Security Act, 1971 — repealed in 1978 after the Emergency — the National Security Act, 1980, and State laws such as the Goondas Acts and Public Safety Acts. The NSA permits detention up to twelve months on grounds prejudicial to India's security, foreign relations, public order or essential supplies and services. Preventive detention was used massively during the 1975-77 Emergency, when tens of thousands were jailed under MISA; courts require communication of grounds, an opportunity to make a representation and effective advisory-board review.",
