    content: "Delhi Police is unusual among Indian metropolitan forces in reporting to the Union Ministry of Home Affairs rather than a State government — a consequence of the Constitution's exclusion of 'public order' and 'police' from the Delhi Assembly's domain under Article 239AA. The force, headed by a Commissioner of Police under the Delhi Police Act, 1978, polices the National Capital Territory, with the New Delhi district covering the high-security zone around Parliament, Rashtrapati Bhavan and the central secretariat, where permissions for assemblies are stringently regulated. The Commissionerate system dates from 1978, superseding the structure inherited from the Police Act, 1861. Because the elected Delhi government has no control over its police, protest permissions and the policing of demonstrations in the capital are effectively Union-domain decisions — a structural fact repeatedly litigated and visible from Ramlila Maidan 2011 to the farmers' border sit-ins of 2020-21.",
