    title: "Essential Services Maintenance Act — strikes in essential services",
    content: "The Essential Services Maintenance Act, 1968 empowers the Union to declare services essential and prohibit strikes in them where interruption would endanger public safety, health or the life of the community, the sovereignty and integrity of India, or the security of the State. Once a service is notified, persons commencing or participating in a strike commit an offence, with harsher penalties for instigators and those financing strikes, and employers may order dismissal. Many States have parallel ESMA statutes. In T. K. Rangarajan v. Government of Tamil Nadu (2003), arising from the mass dismissal of striking government employees and teachers, the Supreme Court held that government employees possess no fundamental, legal or moral right to strike, however legitimate their grievances. Railways, posts, electricity, water, hospitals and transport are commonly notified as essential. ESMA exemplifies Article 19(4)'s reasonable restrictions and remains central to strike politics in India.",
