    content: "The Right to Information movement grew from the Mazdoor Kisan Shakti Sangathan, founded in 1990 at Devdungri in Rajasthan by Aruna Roy with Nikhil Dey and Shankar Singh, fighting corruption in famine-relief and public works. Its method — the jan sunwai, or public hearing — audited official records against villagers' testimonies, exposing wage fraud and ghost entries. MKSS dharnas demanding access to panchayat records won Rajasthan's right-to-information law in 2000, and the National Campaign for People's Right to Information carried the demand nationally. The Freedom of Information Act, 2002 remained unimplemented; after sustained advocacy, Parliament passed the Right to Information Act, 2005, in force from 12 October 2005, covering all public authorities, mandating proactive disclosure and thirty-day replies, with the Central Information Commission hearing appeals. RTI disclosures have exposed scams and empowered citizens; RTI activists have faced violence, and the movement remains a landmark of participatory democracy.",
